-- One click: a folder of night frames in, the finished picture out.
--
-- Everything in this program was learned on real skies, most of it
-- the hard way. The frames are registered on their own stars, with
-- the roll rescue for a tripod somebody touched; the clouded ones are
-- set aside; the rest are stacked sigma-clipped in light, exposures
-- normalised, so a six-second frame and a three-second frame are the
-- same measurement before they meet.
--
-- Then the program refuses to continue unless the stack passes the
-- photographer's own test, the one that caught what every metric
-- missed: a star must appear ONCE. A frame that registered on a
-- handful of coincidental stars fails the confidence gate before it
-- can ghost the stack, and a stack whose stars come out more oval
-- than a single frame's is not developed at all.
--
-- Only then is it developed, by measurement rather than taste: the
-- night start read off this very stack's sky, the stretch lifting
-- luminance alone so the stars keep their own colour, a breath of
-- vibrance for the palest of them, and one gentle black level --
-- last, once, early, because every ruined picture in a week of
-- comparisons was ruined by a clip.
--
-- Deterministic end to end: no model is asked for anything.

param photos: text = "."
param pattern: text = "*.RAF"
param focal_mm: num = 0
param sensor_mm: num = 23.5
param darks: text = ""
param flats: text = ""
param bias: text = ""
param sigma: num = 2.5
param colour: num = 100
param glow_level: num = 18
param flatten: num = 100
param velvet: num = 0
param scale: num = 2
param pixfrac: num = 0.8
param mode: text = "clipped"
param output: text = ""
param only: text = ""
param demosaic: bool = true

use python "night_show_kernel.py"

-- Where every frame sits, by its own stars and the sky's own clock.
placed := register(photos, pattern, only, focal_mm, sensor_mm, demosaic)

-- The frames the sky ruined are set aside before they can vote.
screened := screen_frames(placed)
print(screen_note(screened))

-- Every frame going in must have registered CONVINCINGLY. A stack is
-- only as honest as its least honest frame, and a placement carried
-- by a handful of coincidental stars is how a ghost begins.
check frames_confident(screened)

-- The frames laid on each other, in light, exposures normalised.
stacked := superimpose(screened, mode, output, darks, sigma, flats, bias, scale, pixfrac)
check stack_valid(stacked)
print(stack_note(stacked))

-- The photographer's test: photograph the same sky many times,
-- superimpose, and a star must appear once. A stack whose stars come
-- out longer than a single frame's did not line up, whatever its
-- other numbers say, and it is not developed.
check stack_true(stacked, pattern)

-- Developed by measurement: the sky read off the stack itself, the
-- stars keeping their own colour on the way up.
shown := develop_show(stacked, screened, pattern, colour, glow_level, flatten, velvet)
print(show_note(shown))

-- And the finished picture must keep its blacks and tell the truth:
-- almost nothing clipped, and what it shows as stars verified against
-- the stack it came from.
where := show_home(shown)
check shown_well(shown)
act file.overwrite(where, shown)
settle until check file_exists(where) within 5
commit(shown)
